ack><floats-group><fig id="fig1" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Abdominal CT scanning on day 9 of admission (day 5 after symptoms) showing peritoneal effusion, severely dilated small bowel, and suspected intussusception of the distal Ilium.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.SURGERY2013-510701.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Abdominal CT scanning on day 19 (day 15 after symptoms) showing severely dilated small bowel and characteristic intussusception features: &#x0201c;target lesion&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;doughnut sign&#x0201d; and sausage-shaped mass.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.SURGERY2013-510701.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Intraoperative findings showed intussusception at ileocecal junction with perforation of small bowel.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.SURGERY2013-510701.003"/></fig></floats-group></article>